 Chapter 2 Tests for proportions and means We can use a one-sample test when we want to compare a sample mean to a population mean or a sample proportion to a population proportion. We can use a two-sample test of groups to compare two samples. 2.1 One-sample test of proportions Acock compares adult opinions on school prayer from the 2006 General Social Survey. We will use the recode command to convert 2 into 0 and have a binary \([0,1]\) variable. We will use the tabulate command to make sure our recode worked. cd &quot;/Users/Sam/Desktop/Econ 643/Data/agis6r/&quot; use &quot;gss2002_chapter7.dta&quot;, clear recode prayer (1 = 1 Approve) (2 = 0 Disapprove), gen(schpray) tabulate schpray prayer, missing /Users/Sam/Desktop/Econ 643/Data/agis6r (518 differences between prayer and schpray) RECODE of | prayer | (bible | prayer in | public | bible prayer in public schools schools) | approve disapprov . | Total -----------+---------------------------------+---------- Disapprove | 0 518 0 | 518 Approve | 341 0 0 | 341 . | 0 0 1,906 | 1,906 -----------+---------------------------------+---------- Total | 341 518 1,906 | 2,765 Next we will test the null hypothesis that half of the people approve of school prayer. \[ H_{0}: p=0.5 \] \[ H_{A}: p 
eq 0.5 \] We will test this hypothesis with the prtest command with a two-tailed test. This will do a test of proportions for a one-group sample. The one-sample test of proportion compares a sample to a defined population mean. prtest schpray == 0.5 One-sample test of proportion Number of obs = 859 ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ Variable | Mean Std. err. [95% conf. interval] -------------+---------------------------------------------------------------- schpray | .3969732 .0166937 .3642542 .4296923 ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ p = proportion(schpray) z = -6.0392 H0: p = 0.5 Ha: p &lt; 0.5 Ha: p != 0.5 Ha: p &gt; 0.5 Pr(Z &lt; z) = 0.0000 Pr(|Z| &gt; |z|) = 0.0000 Pr(Z &gt; z) = 1.0000 Our sample mean is \( ar{p} \approx 0.4\). We reject the null hypothesis that half of the population approves of school prayer at the \(0.1\%\) level. 2.2 Two-sample tests of proportions We may want to compare proportions between two samples. Our prior example used SAT scores as an outcome of interest, where the treatment was a SAT preparation program. We saw that pre-treatment pre-SAT scores were similar between treatment and control. Now we want to compare the proportion of students who scored above \(1,200\). Let’s look at our treatment and control again. tab treatment treatment | Freq. Percent Cum. ------------+----------------------------------- 0 | 50 50.00 50.00 1 | 50 50.00 100.00 ------------+----------------------------------- Total | 100 100.00 We can check the null hypothesis that the proportion of treatment students, who scored higher than a \(1,200\), was the same proportion as control students. \[ H_{0}: p_{treatment} - p_{control} = 0 \] \[ H_{A}: p_{treatment} - p_{control} 
