. Let’s find the sample mean of working hours from the 2006 General Social Survey. cd &quot;/Users/Sam/Desktop/Econ 643/Data/agis6r/&quot; use &quot;gss2002_chapter7.dta&quot;, clear summarize hrs1 if wrkstat==1 /Users/Sam/Desktop/Econ 643/Data/agis6r Variable | Obs Mean Std. dev. Min Max -------------+--------------------------------------------------------- hrs1 | 1,419 45.97111 11.88977 4 89 We can see that \( ar{x} = 46\) and \(s = 11.9\). Let’s test to see if this is statistically significant different from the hypothesis of a population mean of 40. \[ H_{0}: \mu_{(hours)}=40 \] \[ H_{A}: \mu_{(hours)} 
eq 40 \] ttest hrs1==40 if wrkstat==1 One-sample t test ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ Variable | Obs Mean Std. err. Std. dev. [95% conf. interval] ---------+-------------------------------------------------------------------- hrs1 | 1,419 45.97111 .3156328 11.88977 45.35195 46.59026 ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ mean = mean(hrs1) t = 18.9179 H0: mean = 40 Degrees of freedom = 1418 Ha: mean &lt; 40 Ha: mean != 40 Ha: mean &gt; 40 Pr(T &lt; t) = 1.0000 Pr(|T| &gt; |t|) = 0.0000 Pr(T &gt; t) = 0.0000 We find that \(t=18.9179\) and \(p&lt;0.001\). We reject the null hypothesis at the \(0.01\%\) level using a two-tailed \(t\)-test that the population mean. In addition, Stata provides a \(95\%\) confidence interval of \([45.3,46.6]\). We find that the average working hours is greater than our hypothesized 40 work week. 2.4 Two-Sample test of group means We can compare the means between two samples using the ttest command with the by option. Acock (2023) uses the example of earnings from men and women. We’ll use the 2006 General Social Survey to assess the group means. Let’s take a look at the income data first. tabulate rincom98 respondents | income | Freq. Percent Cum. ----------------+----------------------------------- under $1 000 | 44 1.59 1.59 $1 000 to 2 999 | 69 2.50 4.09 $3 000 to 3 999 | 37 1.34 5.42 $4 000 to 4 999 | 24 0.87 6.29 $5 000 to 5 999 | 37 1.34 7.63 $6 000 to 6 999 | 20 0.72 8.35 $7 000 to 7 999 | 26 0.94 9.29 $8 000 to 9 999 | 60 2.17 11.46 $10000 to 12499 | 78 2.82 14.29 $12500 to 14999 | 85 3.07 17.36 $15000 to 17499 | 69 2.50 19.86 $17500 to 19999 | 86 3.11 22.97 $20000 to 22499 | 100 3.62 26.58 $22500 to 24999 | 81 2.93 29.51 $25000 to 29999 | 140 5.06 34.58 $30000 to 34999 | 166 6.00 40.58 $35000 to 39999 | 123 4.45 45.03 $40000 to 49999 | 170 6.15 51.18 $50000 to 59999 | 136 4.92 56.09 $60000 to 74999 | 95 3.44 59.53 $75000 - $89999 | 41 1.48 61.0